| 6 | what-happened | Barbosa da Cunha entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. On September 26, 2025, officers arrested him while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. An immigration judge denied a bond hearing. A district court then ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days, and an immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk. The question the government put to the Court is whether the detention statute requires holding people in that situation for the removal proceedings. Its memorandum says an applicant for admission may not be released except by parole. By the grant, the dispute was no longer his case alone, or only the Fifth Circuit and the Eighth Circuit, the two appeals courts whose divided panels the April opinion said had agreed with the government. | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: lived in the United States for more than twenty years after entering the country; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: applied for asylum in 2016 and was granted a valid work permit.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Petitioner requested a bond hearing, which an immigration judge denied based on a determination that he was subject to mandatory detention; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: The district court granted the petition and ordered the government to either provide a bond hearing or release Petitioner within ten days.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: presents no danger to persons or property or risk of flight, released him on bond.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01: Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole.; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: divided panels in two other circuits have agreed with the government; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Buenrostro-Mendez v. Bondi, 166 F.4th 494, 508 (5th Cir. 2026); data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Avila v. Bondi, 170 F.4th 1128, 1138 (8th Cir. 2026); data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: The First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits concluded that Section 1226 applies to noncitizens inside our country; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: The Fifth and Eighth Circuits ruled, on the other hand, that Section 1225 applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted to the United States; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: they must be detained without bond; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt |
