| 23 | where-this-sits | On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit affirmed the district court. It held that detention of a person in the United States after entering without inspection and admission, who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter, follows the bond statute, not mandatory detention. Judge Cabranes agreed and filed a separate opinion. The appeal was argued April 6. | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Decided: April 28, 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Argued: April 6, 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Judge Cabranes concurs in the judgment and opinion of the Court and files a separate opinion.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt |
| 24 | where-this-sits | In July 2025 the government changed its practice. The opinion cites immigration enforcement guidance dated July 8, 2025. The petition names that guidance as Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, and says applicants for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Divided panels in the Fifth and Eighth Circuits agreed with the government. | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025); data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025).; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: may not be released from ICE custody” except by “parole”; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Buenrostro-Mendez v. Bondi, 166 F.4th 494, 508 (5th Cir. 2026); data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Avila v. Bondi, 170 F.4th 1128, 1138 (8th Cir. 2026); data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt |
| 25 | where-this-sits | On September 25, 2026, the Second Circuit denied rehearing by the full court. Judges Bianco and Nathan, joined by four others, concurred in that denial. Judge Menashi dissented. | ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25: rehearing in banc is hereby DENIED.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25.txt<br>ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25: dissents by opinion from the denial of rehearing in banc.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25.txt |
| 26 | where-this-sits | On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals held that immigration judges lack authority to hear bond requests, or to grant bond, for people present in the United States without admission. | bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05: Decided September 5, 2025; data/sources/immigration-detention/bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05.txt<br>bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05: lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission.; data/sources/immigration-detention/bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05.txt |
| 27 | where-this-sits | The signed memorandum, a one-page scan, is addressed from Commissioner Rodney S. Scott. It says the department's position is that applicants for admission are subject to mandatory detention and may not be released except by parole. | cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: FROM: Rodney S. Scott; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: SUBJECT: Detention of Applicants for Admission; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: subject to mandatory detention under INA; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt |
| 28 | unknowns | This case has a grant date of October 1 and no argument day on the list of cases set for argument. The October 2 docket's last disposition is the grant, and later filings go through the electronic system. The question the Court accepted is the statute. The government's petition says a petition from the Sixth Circuit in Lopez-Campos also presents whether applying the detention statute comports with due process. The Second Circuit said it need not rely on constitutional avoidance, the practice of reading a statute so as not to decide a constitutional problem. The petition says the Second Circuit did not reach a standalone holding on the constitutionality of detaining him without a bond hearing. A petition from the Fifth Circuit in Buenrostro-Mendez was distributed for the September 28 conference, then rescheduled, and those proceedings list no grant. The Sixth Circuit petition was distributed for that same conference, and its proceedings list no grant. | scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01: RHONEY V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01: Granted: 10/1/26; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02: Petition GRANTED.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02.txt<br>scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02: Subsequent party and amicus filings in the case should now be submitted through; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02.txt<br>scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01: Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: In addition to the statutory question presented in this case, Lopez-Campos also squarely presents the question whether applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s mandatory-detention scheme to such aliens comports with due process.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: we need not rely here upon the doctrine of constitutional avoidance.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: the Second Circuit did not reach a standalone holding on the constitutionality of detaining respondent without a bond hearing; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-03: United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-03.txt<br>scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-03: DISTRIBUTED for Conference of 9/28/2026.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-03.txt<br>scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03: United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03.txt<br>scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03: DISTRIBUTED for Conference of 9/28/2026.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03.txt<br>scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03: Rescheduled.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03.txt |
