29:2 INTRODUCTION In the decision below, the Second Circuit determined that 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A), which mandates the detention without bond hearings of certain aliens while their removal proceedings unfold, does not apply to aliens who are present in the United States for some time after having entered the country unlawfully. The Second Circuit’s decision conflicts with decisions of the Fifth and Eighth Circuits, both of which have held that aliens present without admission are subject to Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s detention mandate. In Raycraft v. LopezCampos, petition for cert. pending, No. 25-1415 (filed June 22, 2026), the government filed a petition for a writ of certiorari asking this Court to resolve that conflict. The Court should hold the petition in this case pending the resolution of the petition in Lopez-Campos and then dispose of this petition as appropriate. STATEMENT A. Legal Background The Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), 8 U.S.C. 1101 et seq., authorizes the removal of certain classes of aliens from the United States. See 8 U.S.C. 1182, 1227. Various provisions of the INA further authorize the detention of certain aliens pending their removal proceedings. See, e.g., 8 U.S.C. 1225, 1226. This case concerns several INA provisions addressing removal and detention that were enacted in 1996 as part of the Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act (IIRIRA), Pub. L. No. 104-208, Div. C, 110 Stat. 3009-546. 1. Before 1996, the applicable procedures for detaining and expelling an alien under the INA depended largely on whether the alien had physically “entered” the United States, regardless of whether that entry had been lawful. “[E]xpelling an alien who had already en-
30-
31---- page 10 ---
32-3 tered required a deportation proceeding.” Jama v. ICE, 543 U.S. 335, 349 (2005). Aliens in deportation proceedings could be released on bond while those proceedings unfolded. 8 U.S.C. 1252(a)(1) (1994). By contrast, expelling an alien seeking to physically enter the United States “could be achieved through the more summary exclusion proceeding.” Jama, 543 U.S. at 349. The INA mandated that all such aliens arriving at a port of entry—except those “clearly and beyond a doubt entitled” to enter—“shall be detained” for further inquiry, unless they were granted parole. Antiterrorism and Effective Death Penalty Act of 1996, Pub. L. No. 104-132, § 422(a), 110 Stat. 1271; 8 U.S.C. 1225(b) (1994); see 8 U.S.C. 1182(d)(5)(A) (1994). Under that physical-entry-based regime, aliens who managed to evade detection and physically “enter” the United States illegally were subject to deportation proceedings and eligible for bond. 8 U.S.C. 1252(a)(1) (1994). Conversely, aliens who presented themselves for inspection at a port of entry on American soil were deemed not to have “entered” the United States. See Leng May Ma v. Barber, 357 U.S. 185, 188-190 (1958). Those aliens therefore were subject to exclusion proceedings, and to mandatory detention during such proceedings. See 8 U.S.C. 1225(a) and (b) (1994). Thus, aliens who illegally “entered the United States without inspection gain[ed] equities and privileges in immigration proceedings that [we]re not available to aliens who present[ed] themselves for inspection at a port of entry.” H.R. Rep. No. 469, 104th Cong., 2d Sess. Pt. 1, at 225 (1996) (House Report). 2. In IIRIRA, Congress acted to correct that disparity. Congress replaced the defined term “entry” with the term “ ‘admission,’ ” which it defined as “the lawful
33-
34---- page 11 ---
--
39-
40---- page 13 ---
41-6 of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained for” a removal proceeding under Section 1229a. 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A). Though Section 1225(b)(2)(A) speaks in mandatory language, the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) has the discretion to release “any alien applying for admission to the United States” on parole, albeit “only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit.” 8 U.S.C. 1182(d)(5)(A). Section 1226. Section 1226 addresses the arrest and detention of aliens more generally, without limitation to applicants for admission. Unlike Section 1225, detention under Section 1226 is discretionary for many aliens; only certain classes of criminal aliens are subject to mandatory detention under Section 1226. Specifically: • Section 1226(a) authorizes the arrest and detention of aliens “pending a decision on whether the alien is to be removed from the United States.” 8 U.S.C. 1226(a). Such detention is discretionary; the government “may continue to detain the arrested alien” or “may release the alien” on “bond” or “conditional parole.” 8 U.S.C. 1226(a)(1) and (2). By regulation, the alien is entitled to a “custody redetermination” (a bond hearing) before an immigration judge, which can be appealed to the Board of Immigration Appeals. 8 C.F.R. 236.1(d), 1003.19, 1236.1(d)(1). • Section 1226(c) mandates detention for certain criminal aliens. In IIRIRA, Congress required the
42-
43---- page 14 ---
44:7 detention of any alien who is inadmissible or deportable because he (1) committed certain covered offenses, or (2) engaged in terrorism-related activities. § 303(a), 110 Stat. 3009-585 to 3009-586; see Nielsen v. Preap, 586 U.S. 392, 398-399 (2019). Recently, Congress amended Section 1226(c) to require the detention of additional categories of criminal aliens. See Laken Riley Act, Pub. L. No. 119-1, § 2, 139 Stat. 3 (8 U.S.C. 1226(c)(1)(E)). Section 1226(c) prohibits the release of covered criminal aliens except for narrow, witness-protection purposes. 8 U.S.C. 1226(c)(4). 4. After IIRIRA was enacted, the government continued to treat aliens who were present in the United States without having been admitted as eligible for bond hearings—just as they had been before IIRIRA’s comprehensive immigration reforms. See 8 U.S.C. 1252(a)(1) (1994). The preamble to interim rules promulgated in 1997 announced: “Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled * * * will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.” 62 Fed. Reg. 10,312, 10,323 (Mar. 6, 1997). The preamble did not explain why Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s new mandatory-detention regime did not apply to such aliens, whom the government recognized were “applicants for admission.” Ibid. The regulations then contradicted that preamble, stating that Section 1225(b)(2)(A) did apply to certain aliens who were physically present in the United States after an unlawful entry. See 8 C.F.R. 235.3(b)(1)(ii). Even so, the government continued to grant bond hearings to aliens present without admission. In July 2025, DHS revisited the issue and determined that its prior approach was inconsistent with the
45-
46---- page 15 ---
47:8 INA’s text. See Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025). DHS concluded that “applicants for admission” are “subject to detention under [Section 1225(b)] and may not be released from ICE custody” except by “parole” under Section 1182(d)(5). Ibid. (emphasis omitted). As a result, except for aliens subject to Section 1226(c), the “only aliens eligible for a custody determination and release on recognizance, bond, or other conditions under [Section 1226(a)] are aliens admitted to the United States and chargeable with deportability under [Section 1227].” Ibid. (emphasis omitted). The Board of Immigration Appeals reached the same conclusion in a published opinion. In re Yajure Hurtado, 29 I. & N. Dec. 216 (2025). B. Factual And Procedural Background 1. Respondent is a native and citizen of Brazil who entered the United States illegally around 2004 or 2005 and has remained in the country since then. App., infra, 7a. In September 2025, ICE officers encountered respondent at a traffic stop in Norwood, Massachusetts, determined that he had entered the country without inspection or admission, and took respondent into custody. Id. at 7a, 73a. DHS officers initiated removal proceedings against respondent, charging him with being inadmissible under 8 U.S.C. 1182(a)(6)(A)(i), for being present in the United States without being admitted or paroled, and being inadmissible under 8 U.S.C. 1182(a)(7)(A)(i)(I), for not possessing valid immigration documents. App., infra, 8a. DHS determined that respondent must be detained for the duration of his removal proceedings pursuant to Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s detention mandate. Respondent
48-
49---- page 16 ---
50-9 requested a bond hearing, which the immigration judge denied on the grounds that respondent was subject to mandatory detention without the possibility of bond under Section 1225(b)(2)(A). See App., infra, 8a. 2. Since the government implemented its revised interpretation of Section 1225(b)(2)(A), thousands of aliens who are present in the country without having been admitted have filed petitions for habeas corpus seeking release from detention or a bond hearing under 8 U.S.C. 1226(a). Respondent is among that group. See App., infra, 70a. The day he was detained, he filed a habeas petition alleging that his detention without a bond hearing violated the INA and the Fifth Amendment’s Due Process Clause. Ibid. Although respondent initially filed his habeas petition in the United States District Court for the District of Massachusetts, his case was transferred to the United States District Court for the Western District of New York on the stipulation of the parties after respondent was moved to an immigration detention facility in Buffalo, New York. Ibid. The Western District of New York district court granted respondent’s habeas petition. App., infra, 69a85a. The court held that Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to respondent, and that instead his detention is governed by Section 1226(a), which permits release on bond or conditional parole while an alien’s removal proceedings unfold. Id. at 84a. In particular, the court determined that the phrase “ ‘seeking admission’ ” in Section 1225(b)(2)(A) “involves active efforts to obtain ‘admission,’ ” thus limiting the provision to aliens actively trying to obtain lawful entry after inspection by an immigration official. Id. at 79a. In the court’s view, this interpretation was necessary to avoid rendering language in Section 1225(b)(2)(A) superfluous. Id. at 80a.
51-
52---- page 17 ---
53-10 Because the court ruled for respondent on his statutory claim, it declined to reach his alternative argument that detention under Section 1225(b)(2)(A) would violate his due-process rights. Id. at 84a. The district court ordered the government to hold a bond hearing within ten days of its initial text order granting respondent’s habeas petition. App., infra, 84a. A bond hearing was held on October 28, 2025, and respondent was released on bond the next day. Id. at 84a85a. The government then appealed. 3. The court of appeals affirmed. App., infra, 1a68a. The court concluded that Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s detention mandate covers only “those noncitizens who present themselves at a port of entry for admission, or who cross the physical border into the United States but are apprehended at the ‘threshold of initial entry.’ ” Id. at 17a (citation omitted); see id. at 7a (interpreting Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to cover “noncitizens who are apprehended for removal proceedings while entering the country or shortly thereafter”). The court interpreted the phrase “ ‘seeking admission’ ” in Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to refer to the process of “requesting” “lawful entry into the United States after inspection and authorization.” Id. at 16a-17a. Like the district court, the court of appeals found its interpretation of that phrase necessary to avoid surplusage. Id. at 16a. The court also rejected the government’s arguments based on statutory context, structure, and history. Id. at 26a-45a. It asserted that its interpretation was supported by this Court’s decision in Jennings v. Rodriguez, 583 U.S. 281 (2018), because that decision described Section 1225 as operating “at the Nation’s borders and ports of entry.” App., infra, 36a (quoting Jennings, 583 U.S. at 287) (emphasis omitted).
54-
55---- page 18 ---
56:11 The court of appeals further explained that “[e]ven assuming” that the statute is ambiguous, prior Executive Branch practice “strongly counsels against adopting” the government’s interpretation of Section 1225(b)(2)(A). App., infra, 50a; see id. at 50a-55a. Finally, the court reasoned, were the statute ambiguous, constitutional-avoidance principles would support respondent given the court’s “grave” concern that applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to aliens in respondent’s shoes would violate due process. Id. at 55a; see id. at 55a-59a. Judge Cabranes concurred separately “to emphasize key points.” App., infra, 61a; see id. at 61a-68a. REASONS FOR GRANTING THE PETITION This case presents the question whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention of aliens like respondent who are present in the United States without having been admitted. The government recently filed a petition for a writ of certiorari in another case involving the same question, Raycraft v. Lopez-Campos, petition for cert. pending, No. 25-1415 (filed June 22, 2026). This Court should hold this petition pending the disposition of the petition in Lopez-Campos and then dispose of this petition as appropriate. As the government explained in Lopez-Campos, the courts of appeals are divided as to the lawfulness of applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to aliens present in the country without admission, such as after an illegal entry. The Fifth and Eighth Circuits have correctly held that Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s plain language covers such aliens and mandates their detention pending removal proceedings. Buenrostro-Mendez v. Bondi, 166 F.4th 494 (5th Cir. 2026), petition for cert. pending, No. 26-43 (filed July 2, 2026); Avila v. Bondi, 170 F.4th 1128 (8th Cir. 2026). The Second Circuit below, along with the
57-
58---- page 19 ---
59:12 Sixth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits, have held that such aliens are not covered by Section 1225(b)(2)(A) but rather subject to discretionary detention under Section 1226(a) and entitled to bond hearings. See App., infra, 60a; Lopez-Campos v. Raycraft, 175 F.4th 713 (6th Cir. 2026), petition for cert. pending, No. 25-1415 (filed June 22, 2026); Santillan Quiroz v. Mullin, No. 26-6019, 2026 WL 1876709 (10th Cir. June 30, 2026); Hernandez Alvarez v. Warden, 175 F.4th 1258 (11th Cir. 2026).1 Lopez-Campos is a better vehicle than this case for resolving the lawfulness of applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to aliens who are present in the country without having been admitted. In addition to the statutory question presented in this case, Lopez-Campos also squarely presents the question whether applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s mandatory-detention scheme to such aliens comports with due process. As the government explained in its petition in Lopez-Campos, aliens around the country have been challenging their detention under Section 1225(b)(2)(A) on both statutory and due-process grounds, so the Court should address both issues simultaneously to provide clarity to the lower courts. See Pet. at 32-34, 1 At the time the government filed its petition in Lopez-Campos, the Tenth Circuit had not yet decided the issue. Since that filing, the Tenth Circuit has joined the Second, Sixth, and Eleventh Circuits in finding Section 1225(b)(2)(A) inapplicable to aliens present without admission. Santillan Quiroz, 2026 WL 1876709, at *17. In addition, since the government filed its petition in Lopez-Campos, it has filed a petition for hearing en banc in the Eleventh Circuit, asking for the court to vacate the panel decision in Hernandez Alvarez so that the government can at least be relieved of that court’s erroneous decision while the issue pends before this Court. See C.A. Pet. for Reh’g, Hernandez Alvarez, supra (No. 25-14075). That rehearing petition is still pending.
60-
61---- page 20 ---
62:13 Lopez-Campos, supra (No. 25-1415).2 In this case, the Second Circuit addressed due process in the context of constitutional avoidance—underscoring that the statutory and constitutional issues are inextricably linked. However, the Second Circuit did not reach a standalone holding on the constitutionality of detaining respondent without a bond hearing under Section 1225(b)(2)(A). See App., infra, 55a-59a. Lopez-Campos more directly presents the due-process issue, making it a superior vehicle for considering the lawfulness of the government’s mandatory detention pending removal proceedings of aliens present without admission under Section 1225(b)(2)(A). 2 After the government filed its petition in Lopez-Campos, a panel of the Fifth Circuit held that as a matter of procedural due process, “the Government may detain aliens under Section 1225(b)(2)(A) for ninety days but no longer without a bond hearing.” Sosnava Rodriguez v. Ortega, No. 26-50183, 2026 WL 1906557, at *16 (July 2, 2026). The Fifth Circuit then sua sponte ordered rehearing en banc and vacated the panel decision. Sosnava Rodriguez v. Ortega, No. 2650183, 2026 WL 2014647, at *1 (July 10, 2026).
